{#
	The pager, in one place (XIV-69).

	Both pages that page — the record list and a record's history — drew the whole
	range, `{% for page in 1..pages %}`, which is one link per 25 records: forty
	links at a thousand, four hundred at ten thousand, wrapping over several rows
	with the current page lost somewhere inside them. It was also the wrong control
	even when it fitted, because nobody navigating four hundred pages wants to pick
	the two hundred and seventeenth out of a list.

	They were nine near-identical lines each, which is how one of them gets fixed
	and the other does not. This is the one implementation.

	**The window does not change width.** It would be easy to grow the range at the
	ends — show 1–3 on page one, 1–5 by page three — and the effect is a control
	that resizes as somebody moves through it, which is most of what makes a pager
	feel broken. `first` below is clamped so that the window is always `size` wide
	wherever it sits, right up until there are fewer pages than that.

	**The ends disable rather than disappear**, for the same reason: a control that
	changes its number of buttons moves everything beside it, and on the list that
	is the record count sitting to its left.

	**The caller supplies the parameters, not just the route.** The two pages build
	their links differently and both are right: the list merges the current query
	string, which is what keeps a filter and a sort alive across a page change, and
	the history has a record id and no query to keep. A macro that only took a
	route would have had to know about one of those.
#}

{#
	@param route   the route each page links to
	@param params  everything that route needs except `page` — for the list, the
	               current query string merged with the module key
	@param current the page being shown, from 1
	@param pages   how many there are
	@param label   what a screen reader calls this control
	@param size    how many numbered pages to show at once; odd, so the current
	               one sits in the middle
#}
{% macro pager(route, params, current, pages, label, size = 5) %}
	{% if pages > 1 %}
		{# Clamped twice: not past the start, and not so far right that the window
		   would run off the end. The second is what keeps the width fixed. #}
		{% set first = max(1, min(current - (size - 1) // 2, pages - size + 1)) %}
		{% set last = min(pages, first + size - 1) %}

		<nav aria-label="{{ label }}">
			<ul class="pagination pagination-sm mb-0">
				{{ _self.step(route, params, 1, current > 1, 'chevron-double-left', 'module.page_first'|trans) }}
				{{ _self.step(route, params, current - 1, current > 1, 'chevron-left', 'module.page_previous'|trans) }}

				{#
					An ellipsis rather than a link to the pages in between: it says
					the range is a window without offering a destination nobody
					asked for. `aria-hidden` because "First" already leads there.
				#}
				{% if first > 1 %}
					<li class="page-item disabled" aria-hidden="true">
						<span class="page-link">&hellip;</span>
					</li>
				{% endif %}

				{% for page in first..last %}
					<li class="page-item {{ page == current ? 'active' : '' }}">
						<a class="page-link"
						   {{ page == current ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' }}
						   href="{{ path(route, params|merge({page: page})) }}">{{ page }}</a>
					</li>
				{% endfor %}

				{% if last < pages %}
					<li class="page-item disabled" aria-hidden="true">
						<span class="page-link">&hellip;</span>
					</li>
				{% endif %}

				{{ _self.step(route, params, current + 1, current < pages, 'chevron-right', 'module.page_next'|trans) }}
				{{ _self.step(route, params, pages, current < pages, 'chevron-double-right', 'module.page_last'|trans) }}
			</ul>
		</nav>
	{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{#
	One of the four step buttons. Disabled is a `<span>` rather than an `<a>` with
	a class, because a link with no destination is still a link to a keyboard and
	to a screen reader; Bootstrap's `.disabled` only greys it.

	The glyph is decorative and the name is the accessible one, which is why the
	icon is `aria-hidden` and the text is visually hidden rather than absent.
#}
{% macro step(route, params, page, enabled, icon, label) %}
	{% if enabled %}
		<li class="page-item">
			<a class="page-link" href="{{ path(route, params|merge({page: page})) }}" aria-label="{{ label }}">
				<i class="bi bi-{{ icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
			</a>
		</li>
	{% else %}
		<li class="page-item disabled">
			<span class="page-link" aria-disabled="true">
				<i class="bi bi-{{ icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span class="visually-hidden">{{ label }}</span>
			</span>
		</li>
	{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
